<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revolución Libertadora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Liberating Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Revolución Libertadora, or Liberating Revolution, was the military coup of September 1955 that overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a> and the military government that followed until 1958, led first by Eduardo Lonardi and then by Pedro Eugenio Aramburu. It banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronism">Peronism</a>, repressed a Peronist rising in 1956 with executions, and restored the constitution of 1853.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/revolucion-libertadora.jpg" alt="Revolución Libertadora" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FelipeRev · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Revoluci%C3%B3n_Libertadora_montaje.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Domingo Perón</a>, elected in 1946, was re-elected in 1951 under a constitutional amendment of 1949 that allowed unrestricted re-election. A military rebellion led by Benjamín Menéndez on 28 September 1951 collapsed within half a day. Economic downturns, Perón's personality cult, the suppression of the press, the harassment and exile of dissidents and a growing conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and the armed forces' leadership eroded his support. On 14 June 1955 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> procession turned into an anti-government demonstration.</p><p>Two days later, on 16 June 1955, 30 Navy and Air Force aircraft bombed the Plaza de Mayo while a crowd gathered there in support of Perón, killing over 300 civilians in the largest aerial bombing ever carried out on the Argentine mainland. That night extremist Peronist groups burned several churches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 16 September 1955 a new rising led by General Eduardo Lonardi, General Pedro E. Aramburu and Admiral Isaac Rojas began, with Lonardi's command at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, where he declared himself provisional president. Fighting broke out at Córdoba, the naval bases of Puerto Belgrano and Río Santiago and at Curuzú Cuatiá, where Aramburu's rising was at first defeated. Rebel warships blockaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> and on 19 September bombarded Mar del Plata.</p><p>That day Perón wrote an ambiguous letter to the Army minister, General Franklin Lucero, hinting at resignation. A junta of loyal generals under José Domingo Molina treated it as one and negotiated with the rebels aboard a cruiser, agreeing on 21 September that Lonardi would take charge. Seeing the country on the brink of civil war, Perón took shelter aboard the ARP Paraguay and sought asylum in Paraguay. Lonardi was sworn in on 23 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Lonardi promised neither victors nor vanquished, echoing Urquiza after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caseros">Battle of Caseros</a>, but in November hard-liners deposed him in favour of Aramburu. The regime closed Congress, purged the Supreme Court and the universities, accused Perón of treason and forbade any public mention of him or his late wife; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eva-peron">Eva Perón</a>'s remains were taken secretly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> under a false name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> administration aided the new government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronism">Peronism</a> remained proscribed until Perón's return in 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Revolución Libertadora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
